Z||000000||alpo||06-09-2001||07:51 AM||||I just purchased my Fiero today. A 87 GT 5-spd w/69K.  I need rear windows, Dr side seat lower cloth, and the door window lower seals.  The most important thing is it appears the rear spark plugs have been rounded off (all three?)!  Was there any special plugs ever used the need special tools?  Are there any techniques to get these out?  The car runs fine although it appears it hasn't had a tune in quit some time by the condition of the other plugs the cap and rotor (which I replaced). The car is in good condition otherwise (for $2800) and pretty much loaded.  Any info would be appreciated (SN #1G2PG1195HP237278). Any local members in NJ?||||reg||5
Z||000001||SteveJ||06-09-2001||09:20 AM||||Well welcome. Sounds like someone used a twelve point socket on your plugs. Use only six point from this point on. Got any flat edges? Enough to use with a six point? Dose these plugs good with WD40 or a good rust penetrating oil and let 'em soak overnight at least. After that you may need to think about trying the next smaller socket on the plug and driving it on ( GENTLY ) with a hammer.<BR>I know that the ceramic part is held in by a crimp at the top of the plug so remove that and you get a hole right though the plug you can put a screw extractor into but that is really some last ditch stuff and as long as these plugs still work I wouldn't do that.<BR>Let's see what other ideas people come up with.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||alpo||06-09-2001||09:56 AM||||I was using a 6 point socket.  Unfortunately these are the rear plugs (the 3 that face the passenger compartment).  I doubt that I can even get a hammer back there to do any good.  It looks as though the faces of the plug are totally gone, using a mirror at a awkward angle. Fortunately the car does run good, and it looks as though I will be taking it somewhere in the future to get the rear plugs changed.  I just now found out the oil filter is just as hard to get to as the rear plugs.  All well, I will trudge on.  I still like the car, it is actually the wife's - she loves it.....||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jay||06-09-2001||11:54 AM||||Welcome to the forum.  I suggest not taking it to a mechanic to have the rear spark plugs changed.  Usually they leave them in there and just tell you they changed them (it's happened more than once).  Take the rear deck lid off to give yourself some more room, you should be able to get a deep socket on them.  Good Luck<P>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||alpo||06-09-2001||12:39 PM||||I can get the socket to and on them now (not easily).  It is that they just do not have enough bitable surface to get a grip on them.  They look as though they have been in there for a great deal of time, and will most likely be difficult to get out.  I appreciate all the replies, and if anyone has information on a tool that I do not know of please reply.  I do not want to try the hammering idea yet, as the car is still running and am afraid of the possible implications that may result in.  Of all the forums I have seen, this is by far the best.  ALPO.....||||reg||1
Z||000005||cadaver||06-09-2001||01:29 PM||||Welcome to the best fiero based site around. <BR>As for the plugs Hmmm Try some aluminum foil on the plug then the socket. I know it sounds funny but I have done it. I use speed tape which is thicker than foil but it might just give you the extra to get a bit on the plug. But be sure to use a good penetrating oil. I use mouse milk. Its great.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfiero||06-09-2001||01:35 PM||||There might be some crud built up around the plugs which is preventing you from getting a bite with the socket.  Try cleaning up around the plugs with a small screwdriver and see if that helps.<P>BTW......the plugs facing the firewall are the front ones (just an FYI).<P>Good luck with it.....it sounds like a decent GT.   ||||reg||1
Z||000007||DecadenceR||06-09-2001||05:42 PM||||Alpo-<BR>Welcome. I'm from NJ.<BR>E-mail me and I'll put you in touch with a guy local to me (Howell area) that has some parts for sale. He works at the local Pep-boys, and he puts LT1's in cars like yours.||||reg||1
